# Changelog

This file records user-visible changes to the Quake II PlayStation 2 port.
Development priorities and completion criteria are tracked in
[ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md).

## Unreleased

## 0.1.0-alpha.19 - 2026-08-01

### Fixed

- Fixed mipmapped translucent textures sampling unrelated materials at a
  distance. The VU1 alpha packet now programs both `MIPTBP1` and the GS alpha
  equation instead of reusing the single state slot for one or the other.
- Expanded the matching VU1 input/output layout from six to seven GIF-state
  qwords while retaining the existing 96-vertex batch size. The maximum batch
  occupies 495 of the 496 qwords available in each double-buffer half.
- Made every 3D batch self-contained for texture mip addresses and blending
  state, preventing glass from inheriting stale mip pointers from a wall,
  door or other texture previously drawn in the same GS context.

### Validation target

- View the Base 3 glass while walking from close range to the far end of the
  room. Its material and opacity should remain stable across mip transitions.
- Confirm the glass blends with geometry behind it rather than becoming a
  solid grey panel at close range.
- Recheck ordinary mipmapped floors/walls, particles and weapon models because
  they share the expanded VU1 packet layout.

## 0.1.0-alpha.18 - 2026-08-01

### Fixed

- Capped the persistent adaptive BSP-lighting cache at 1.5 MB. Once the
  budget is full, newly visible triangles are lit into the existing static
  scratch buffer and submitted immediately instead of consuming the remaining
  EE heap. This addresses the Base 3 `Mdl_World` allocation failure whose
  final 144-byte request was exactly one cached triangle.
- Restored translucent inline/brush entities such as moving windows and other
  map objects marked `RF_TRANSLUCENT`. The PS2 renderer previously skipped
  those models completely even though the original `ref_gl` draws them at 25%
  opacity.
- Rendered `SURF_TRANS33` and `SURF_TRANS66` faces belonging to inline brush
  models with their own model transform, rather than dropping them from both
  the opaque world pass and the world-space alpha chain.

### Validation target

- Re-enter Base 3 and move through several large views; the `LitKB` diagnostic
  should stop growing near 1536 KB and no `Mdl_World` allocation error should
  occur.
- Check the glass from both sides, including any moving glass/door sections,
  and verify that it blends over the world instead of disappearing.
- Recheck doors and lifts because they share the inline brush-model path.

## 0.1.0-alpha.17 - 2026-08-01

### Added

- Added the first back-to-front pass for ordinary `SURF_TRANS33` and
  `SURF_TRANS66` BSP surfaces, restoring translucent windows and similar map
  geometry while retaining the existing six-plane safety clipper.
- Added actual per-vertex alpha for translucent MD2 entities. The renderer
  previously enabled GS blending for `RF_TRANSLUCENT` but accidentally packed
  every alias vertex with fully opaque alpha.

### Changed

- Render entities in the original Quake II two-pass order: all opaque models
  first, followed by `RF_TRANSLUCENT` models.
- Keep `SURF_WARP` geometry deferred even when it also carries a transparency
  flag. Animated water, lava and slime remain the next renderer milestone.

### Validation target

- Check glass or other translucent map surfaces from both sides and with
  enemies, particles and sky visible behind them.
- Check translucent effects represented by MD2 or sprite entities for proper
  fading instead of opaque rendering.
- Water/lava/slime may still be absent in this build by design.

## 0.1.0-alpha.16 - 2026-08-01

### Fixed

- Moved the far-depth skybox pass before alpha-tested/blended entities and
  particles. Transparent particle texels can no longer block the later sky
  depth test and expose dark clear-colour triangles against the skybox.
- Kept the sky after opaque BSP geometry, so it still fills only untouched
  background and cannot cover walls or other world surfaces.

### Validation target

- Particle trails and impacts viewed against open sky should retain their
  intended small shapes with no dark triangular or square backgrounds.
- Recheck ordinary world occlusion, first-person weapons and the sky seams.

## 0.1.0-alpha.15 - 2026-08-01

### Added

- Added the first PS2 skybox pass using Quake II's original six-face naming,
  face orientation and optional worldspawn rotation axis/speed.
- Visible `SURF_SKY` polygons now act as portals into a camera-centred skybox
  instead of exposing the framebuffer clear colour.
- Reused the renderer's six-plane EE clipper for sky faces, so geometry that
  crosses the camera or VU1 guard band cannot produce stretched triangles.

### Changed

- Draw sky fragments at the far end of the reversed depth range after the
  opaque scene. They fill only untouched background pixels and cannot cover
  BSP geometry, entities, weapons or particles.
- Load sky faces lazily on the first visible sky frame, after level
  registration and its peak BSP allocation have completed.
- Convert opaque TGA sky faces from RGBA8888 to GS-native RGB5A1 at load time.
  A standard six-face 256x256 sky now uses about 768 KB instead of 1.5 MB in
  both EE texture memory and GS VRAM.

### Validation target

- Outdoor openings on `base1` and later maps should show the original
  environment instead of the flat blue/purple clear colour.
- Check face orientation, seams and camera-centred movement while looking in
  every direction; walls, particles and the view weapon must remain intact.
- Recheck a `base1` to `base2` transition after the sky has appeared once and
  watch for allocation failures or first-frame corruption.

## 0.1.0-alpha.14 - 2026-08-01

### Fixed

- Restored the original renderer-to-game `r_lightlevel` signal. The PS2
  renderer now samples BSP lighting at the camera every frame and sends the
  resulting byte through `usercmd_t` to the server-side player entity.
- Fixed monsters treating the player as permanently invisible because the
  previously untouched `r_lightlevel` cvar remained zero. This prevented
  normal visual acquisition until weapon noise or damage alerted them.
- Included dynamic-light contribution and clamped the transmitted value to the
  byte range, avoiding wraparound in very bright scenes.
- Added a `PlayerLit` profiling row so detection input can be validated on
  PCSX2 and real hardware without using the console.

### Expected result

- Monsters should notice a sufficiently lit player in their normal field of
  view without waiting for a shot.
- A monster awakened by weapon noise should switch from its temporary sound
  target to the visible player instead of running into the player and
  repeatedly twitching without selecting an attack.

## 0.1.0-alpha.13 - 2026-08-01

### Fixed

- Skipped the complete 3D render pass during the short level-transition
  interval after alpha.12 frees the old renderer world and before
  `CL_PrepRefresh` registers the new one.
- Fixed `Assert Failed: RenderFrame without a world model!` during the
  `base1` to `base2` loading screen.
- Avoided rendering entities in that interval as well, since their cached
  model pointers belong to the same retired registration cycle. The 2D
  loading plaque continues to render normally.

## 0.1.0-alpha.12 - 2026-08-01

### Fixed

- Freed the previous BSP lighting cache and renderer world hunk at the start
  of an integrated PS2 server level change, before `CM_LoadMap` allocates a
  full-file buffer for the next BSP.
- Eliminated the avoidable old-world/new-file memory overlap that could crash
  both PCSX2 and real hardware while transitioning from `base2` to `base3`.
  The reported 2,337,840-byte failed `Quake` allocation was identified as the
  2,337,824-byte `maps/base3.bsp` plus allocator bookkeeping.
- Cleared inline brush-model aliases when their owning world hunk is purged,
  preventing stale renderer pointers from surviving into registration of the
  next map.

### Performance validation

- Alpha.11 reduced measured `Ent us` by roughly 38% in the starting view,
  24% in the larger model-heavy view and 44% in the supplied combat view.
- `MD2Corner` was approximately 5.3-5.8 times `MD2Vert`, confirming that the
  indexed path avoids substantial repeated interpolation and lighting work.
- The supplied captures remained visually correct on PCSX2 and the same level
  transition failure was reproduced on real hardware, making transition
  memory the alpha.12 priority.

## 0.1.0-alpha.11 - 2026-07-29

### Changed

- Prepared each unique indexed MD2 position and vertex-lighting value once per
  entity instead of repeating interpolation, normal lookup and lighting for
  every triangle corner.
- Expanded ordinary enemy and pickup triangles directly into the VU1 DMA
  buffer while retaining the full six-plane clipped path for first-person
  weapons carrying `RF_DEPTHHACK`.
- Added `MD2Vert` and `MD2Corner` overlay rows. Their ratio shows how much
  repeated per-corner work the indexed preparation avoids in the current
  frame.

### Validation target

- Compare `Ent us` in the same combat scene against alpha.10 while checking
  animated enemies, corpses, pickups and every first-person weapon for visual
  regressions.
- `MD2Vert` should remain substantially below `MD2Corner`; both values are
  frame counters rather than memory usage.

### Performance validation

- Alpha.10 reduced the starting-area BSP phase from 36.3 ms to 10.3 ms and a
  heavy scene from 45.0 ms to 15.3 ms, approximately 72% and 66% respectively.
- Warm-cache captures reported `LitBuild 0`; the cache footprint ranged from
  about 0.35 MB to 2.8 MB across the supplied scenes.
- The new limiting phase is entity/MD2 preparation, reaching approximately
  32 ms in busy combat scenes. Alpha.11 targets that phase without reducing
  geometry or lighting quality.

## 0.1.0-alpha.10 - 2026-07-29

### Added

- Added a camera-independent cache for adaptively tessellated BSP triangles,
  including their diffuse coordinates and final gamma-corrected vertex
  colours. Stable surfaces no longer repeat recursive subdivision and
  bilinear lightmap sampling every frame.
- Keyed each cached surface by only the animated light styles it uses plus the
  lighting-quality settings. Blinking lamps invalidate their own affected
  faces without flushing constant world lighting.
- Added `LitHit`, `LitBuild` and `LitKB` profiling rows for cache reuse,
  rebuilds and EE-memory footprint.
- Freed all renderer-owned cache allocations before replacing the world model
  during map registration.

### Expected result

- After the first visible frame warms the cache, `World us` should fall while
  geometry, lighting, clipping, animated lamps and texture quality remain
  unchanged.
- The first visit to a new area can still build previously unseen entries;
  animated light styles can produce small, targeted rebuild counts.

### Performance baseline

- Alpha.9 heavy combat scene: 11 FPS, 76.0 ms in the 3D pass, with 45.0 ms in
  the BSP/world phase and 30.6 ms in entities.
- Alpha.9 starting area: 15 FPS, 48.8 ms in the 3D pass, with 36.3 ms in the
  BSP/world phase and 11.8 ms in entities.
- VU1, texture-DMA and VRAM-reuse waits were below 0.5 ms in both captures,
  confirming that the current bottleneck is EE-side geometry preparation.

## 0.1.0-alpha.9 - 2026-07-29

### Added

- Added microsecond timing for camera setup, BSP/world rendering, entities,
  particles and the complete 3D view pass to the always-visible draw overlay.
- Added separate counters for time the EE spends waiting on VIF1/VU1, texture
  transfer DMA and full GS stalls required before evicted VRAM can be reused.
- Reset all timing counters automatically at frame start; profiling requires
  no developer console or keyboard.

### Notes

- Phase timings include any VU/texture waits occurring inside the phase.
  Dedicated wait rows expose that overlap so CPU work and pipeline stalls can
  be distinguished from a screenshot.
- This build intentionally changes instrumentation only. It establishes the
  baseline needed to choose the first visual-quality-neutral optimization.

### Validation

- Confirmed in both PCSX2 and on a real PlayStation 2 that alpha.8 removes the
  stretched view-weapon triangles while keeping the railgun intact.
- Confirmed on real hardware that the BSP face triangulation fix remains
  correct and the current renderer looks visually stable.
- Real-hardware performance has become the primary blocker as renderer
  completeness increased. The next development pass is now focused on
  measurable CPU/VU/GS and texture-streaming improvements before sky and audio
  add more load.

## 0.1.0-alpha.8 - 2026-07-29

### Fixed

- Added six-plane EE clipping for first-person MD2 models carrying
  `RF_DEPTHHACK`. Triangles crossing the camera or VU guard band are now cut
  with interpolated texture coordinates and vertex colours instead of
  exploding across the screen after perspective division.
- Kept enemies, pickups and other ordinary MD2 entities on the existing fast
  path so the additional clipping work is limited to the active view weapon.

### Validation

- Confirmed that alpha.7 removes the reported holes in cliff and wall geometry.
- Confirmed that alpha.7 prevents the railgun from being cut off, but exposed
  missing MD2 near-plane clipping as severe stretched weapon triangles.

## 0.1.0-alpha.7 - 2026-07-29

### Fixed

- Replaced the failure-prone generic ear-clipping path with deterministic
  triangle fans for Quake II's convex BSP faces. Numerically awkward cliff and
  rock polygons can no longer leave zeroed triangles and clear-colour wedges.
- Added the original `RF_DEPTHHACK` behaviour for first-person weapons.
  View-models now use a smaller near plane and the nearest 30 percent of the
  depth range, preventing close railgun and BFG geometry from being rejected
  by the VU clipping guard.

### Known limitations

- Sky surfaces and skyboxes are still not rendered. The purple clear colour
  remains visible through real sky openings until the next renderer pass.
- View-weapon clipping is completed by alpha.8.

### Validation

- Confirmed in PCSX2 that alpha.6 renders and animates opaque doors, compound
  door panels, lifts and their brush-based buttons correctly.
- Confirmed that brush textures remain attached during movement and no missing
  faces or new depth artifacts were visible in the supplied scenes.
- Observed approximately 3,000 drawn triangles at 20–30 FPS in the tested
  door and lift areas.

## 0.1.0-alpha.6 - 2026-07-29

### Added

- Added the first opaque inline BSP/brush entity renderer for moving doors,
  lifts, platforms and other map submodels.
- Added per-entity translation and rotation using Quake II's original brush
  pitch/roll convention, local-space backface tests and conservative rotated
  bounds culling.
- Reused animated wall textures, animated light styles, BSP gamma correction,
  adaptive light sampling, clipping, texture batching and VU1 submission for
  brush entities.

### Known limitations

- Translucent, sky and turbulent brush surfaces remain deferred until their
  dedicated sorted rendering passes are implemented.
- Dynamic lights do not yet affect brush surfaces.

### Validation

- Confirmed in PCSX2 that alpha.5 makes the starting indoor area noticeably
  brighter while retaining dark doorways and avoiding visible clipping in
  already bright outdoor areas.
- Observed approximately 4,500–9,200 drawn triangles and 12–15 FPS in the
  supplied scenes. Gamma correction itself adds no geometry; the variation
  comes from adaptive BSP lighting and scene visibility.

## 0.1.0-alpha.5 - 2026-07-29

### Added

- Added a 129-entry gamma lookup table for BSP light coefficients. This
  approximates applying the display curve after `texture × light`, lifting
  dark and middle lightmap values while preserving black and full brightness.
- Added the archived `ps2_world_light_gamma` setting with a console-free
  default of `0.70`; the table is rebuilt only when the value changes.

### Validation

- Confirmed that alpha.4 improves local BSP light variation and remains free
  of the alpha.2 depth corruption.
- Observed roughly 3,700 to 8,000 drawn triangles and 12–20 FPS in the supplied
  PCSX2 scenes. The starting indoor area remained too dark, showing that the
  remaining mismatch was tonal rather than insufficient tessellation.

## 0.1.0-alpha.4 - 2026-07-29

### Changed

- Replaced uniform BSP lightmap-space subdivision with error-driven adaptive
  tessellation. The renderer now compares real midpoint and centroid lightmap
  samples against linearly interpolated vertex light and adds detail only
  where the difference is visible.
- Increased the maximum local subdivision depth from 16 to 64 triangles per
  original triangle while allowing evenly lit surfaces to stop earlier.
- Changed the default coarse light probe spacing to eight lightmap cells and
  added the archived `ps2_light_error` tolerance setting. Both use playable
  defaults and require no console input.

### Expected result

- Large indoor BSP surfaces should retain more local lamp and shadow variation
  without globally increasing gamma or overexposing already bright areas.
- Triangle counts may rise around complex lighting, but should not increase
  uniformly across flat or linearly lit surfaces.

### Validation

- Confirmed in PCSX2 that alpha.3 removes the severe GS depth/rendering
  corruption while particles, first-person weapons and MD2 enemies continue
  to render correctly.
- Confirmed that the revised palette processing improves overall brightness.
  Large indoor BSP surfaces can still lose local light variation because the
  current renderer approximates lightmaps with a bounded vertex-light grid.

## 0.1.0-alpha.3 - 2026-07-29

### Added

- Added separate gamma-only and light-scaled Quake palette CLUTs. Mipmapped
  world and MD2 textures now receive the original renderer's `intensity=2`
  preprocessing, while UI, particles and sprites retain their correct colour
  treatment.
- Added a PS2-oriented startup gamma default that does not require access to
  the developer console. The archived `ps2_gamma` and
  `ps2_texture_intensity` settings are ready for later menu exposure.

### Changed

- Restored the BSP and dynamic-light multiplier to the original renderer's
  `gl_modulate=1` equivalent; texture intensity is now applied at the correct
  stage instead of being folded into light samples.
- Confirmed camera-facing particles and their fading alpha in PCSX2.

### Fixed

- Fixed the severe world and view-model corruption introduced in alpha.2 by
  unsafe per-batch GS Z-buffer programming.
- Restored the proven compact VU1 packet layout while retaining source-alpha
  blending for particles and translucent sprites.

## 0.1.0-alpha.2 - 2026-07-29

### Added

- Added the original Quake II particle dot texture and camera-facing particle
  triangles using the engine-provided palette colors and alpha values.
- Added source-alpha blending and depth-write masking to translucent VU1
  batches, enabling faded particles and translucent SP2 sprite entities.
- Confirmed the first SP2 sprite pass with the BFG projectile in PCSX2.

## 0.1.0-alpha.1 - 2026-07-29

### Added

- Added upload and GS binding of all four mip levels stored in WAL world
  textures, with mipmapped minification filtering.
- Added a first static BSP lighting pass that samples Quake II RGB lightmap
  data at surface vertices and interpolates it through the VU1 draw path.
- Added adaptive lightmap-space tessellation for large BSP surfaces, sampling
  interior RGB lighting without a second GS texture pass. The archived
  `ps2_light_subdivide` cvar controls its detail/performance balance.
- Added frame-driven Quake II light-style scaling to BSP samples, restoring
  animated flicker and pulse patterns from the original level data.
- Confirmed animated BSP light styles and improved interior lighting in PCSX2.
- Added the original Quake II MD2 lighting model: BSP light sampling beneath
  entities, per-vertex normal shading, minimum-light and pulsing-item flags.
- Confirmed in PCSX2 that enemies and first-person weapons correctly respond
  to the surrounding BSP lighting.
- Added original Quake II distance-attenuated dynamic lights to MD2 entities,
  restoring coloured illumination from muzzle flashes and explosions.
- Confirmed muzzle-flash dynamic lighting in PCSX2.
- Changed the default analog layout to a modern FPS scheme: left stick moves
  and strafes, while the right stick controls the camera.
- Added an L3 test shortcut for the single-player `give all` command.
- Added a first camera-facing sprite-entity renderer with animated SP2 frames
  and palette-index transparency.

### Fixed

- Confirmed full-height `640x512` PAL output and CRT framing on a real PS2.
- Rebased repeating BSP texture coordinates per surface to stay inside the
  real GS STQ precision/range limits and reduce texture swimming while moving.
- Tuned world-texture LOD bias so nearby surfaces retain their high-resolution
  mip level instead of appearing excessively blurred.
- Confirmed on real PS2 hardware that the corrected BSP coordinates and WAL
  mipmap LOD eliminate floor/wall shimmer without excessive blur.
- Switched world mipmap minification from trilinear filtering to bilinear
  filtering with nearest-level selection to reduce GS fill cost.
- Matched the original renderer's default light intensity for the BSP vertex
  lighting pass and added the archived `ps2_light_scale` tuning cvar.

### Documentation

- Reactivated the project documentation.
- Added a maintained development roadmap.
- Replaced the legacy free-form changelog with dated, user-visible entries.
- Rebuilt the repository landing page with current feature status, test-build
  downloads, PCSX2/real-hardware setup, controls, build instructions and
  upstream credits.
- Added structured bug and real-hardware test report forms.
- Updated the GitHub repository description, topics and issue-tracker settings.

## 2026-07-28 - First playable renderer test

### Added

- Added a reproducible GitHub Actions build that produces `quake2.elf`.
- Added textured BSP world rendering through the VU1 triangle pipeline.
- Added animated MD2 entity and first-person weapon rendering.
- Added PCX, WAL and TGA loading with a shared 8-bit GS palette.
- Added texture and model registration caches with streamed GS VRAM residency.
- Added DualShock input through `libpad`, including gameplay and menu controls.
- Added PCSX2 `host:` and real-console USB `mass:` game-data discovery.
- Added draw, memory and VRAM diagnostic overlays.
- Added cinematic playback and mixed 2D/3D frame rendering.

### Fixed

- Fixed NPOT MD2 skin coordinates, including the original half-texel offset.
- Fixed MD2 data alignment for the PS2 compiler.
- Fixed real-hardware CLUT uploads by synchronising the EE data cache before DMA.
- Fixed VRAM exhaustion on busy frames by allowing synchronised in-frame texture
  eviction.
- Added full-height PAL output (`640x512`); NTSC remains `640x448`.
- Added EE-side clipping for world triangles crossing the VU1 clip volume.
- Fixed several inline-model, texture-cache and framebuffer/depth-buffer issues.

### Known limitations

- Static BSP and MD2 lighting are implemented; dynamic lights currently affect
  MD2 entities only, while dynamic world lighting and full lightmap rendering
  are not implemented.
- Sound output is not implemented.
- Sprite and particle rendering are in their first validation passes; sky,
  water and brush-entity rendering remain incomplete.
- Save/load and long-play stability on real hardware still need validation.

## Legacy project notes

- Most PS2-specific code lives under `src/ps2/`.
- The original source was reformatted with the repository's `_clang-format`.
- Historical source changes may carry comments prefixed with `LAMPERT`.
- The Hunk allocation API was changed to return a user structure, reducing
  additional global state.
- GCC format checking was added to variadic functions.
